# Portscope — Project Context for Cursor AI

## Role
You are a senior full-stack engineer building Portscope — an AI-powered family
office autopilot service. You have deep expertise in TypeScript/NestJS, Python
ML pipelines, Docker/cloud infrastructure, and financial services domain knowledge
(alternative investments, capital calls, fund administration).

## What Portscope IS
Portscope is NOT a portfolio dashboard. It is an async document processing and
outcome-delivery service that REPLACES the fund administrator for small family
offices (<$500M AUM).

The product:
1. Alternative investment documents arrive by email or manual upload
2. A managed VLM API (Claude Vision / Document AI) extracts structured data
3. Temporal workflows orchestrate review, persistence, and delivery
4. Reconciled quarterly reports are delivered to families by email + portal
5. Humans barely touch any interface. The autopilot does the work.

Business model (Sequoia "Services as Software" thesis):
- We sell OUTCOMES: closed books, processed documents, delivered reports
- We charge per document + monthly retainer per entity under management
- We REPLACE the fund admin ($3-8K/mo), not add to their software stack

## Monorepo structure (pnpm workspaces + Turborepo)
portscope-dev/
├── apps/
│   ├── api/          # NestJS 10 API (Docker → cloud)
│   ├── web/          # Next.js 15 App Router (Vercel)
│   ├── extractor/    # Python FastAPI — VLM extraction (Docker → cloud)
│   └── worker/       # Temporal workflow workers (Docker → cloud)
├── packages/
│   ├── db/           # Prisma 6 schema + generated client
│   ├── shared/       # Shared TypeScript types (ExtractedField, Document, etc.)
│   └── extractor-client/  # TypeScript HTTP client for Python extractor
├── docker-compose.yml
└── turbo.json

## Web app route structure (apps/web)
Two route groups with separate auth:
(review)/ — Internal staff only (staff analyst)
  /queue              → Review Queue: inbox-style, pending docs
  /documents/[id]     → PDF + extracted fields side-by-side
  /reports/[id]       → Draft report approval before delivery to family
(portal)/ — Client read-only (family principal)
  /portal             → Net worth + 4 stats + report download
  /portal/reports     → Report history with PDF downloads
  /portal/funds       → Fund list with IRR/TVPI/DPI
  Entry: magic link email ONLY. No password. 7-day session cookie.

## Tech stack — NEVER deviate without discussion

TypeScript stack (apps/api, apps/web, apps/worker):
- NestJS 10 for the API (not Express, not Fastify directly)
- Prisma 6 ORM (never raw SQL queries, never TypeORM)
- Temporal TypeScript SDK for ALL async workflows
- Next.js 15 App Router (never Pages Router)
- shadcn/ui + Tailwind CSS for UI
- zod for ALL input validation
- Auth.js v5 (magic links for portal, JWT for staff review UI)

Python stack (apps/extractor):
- FastAPI + uvicorn (not Flask, not Django)
- Pydantic v2 for ALL request/response schemas
- Managed VLM API (Claude Vision / Document AI) for extraction
- Docling as primary PDF parser (unstructured as fallback)

Infrastructure:
- Docker Compose for local dev (postgres, temporal, redis)
- All services containerized and cloud-deployable
- PostgreSQL 16 + pgvector via Prisma
- S3-compatible object storage for documents

## Core domain model (Prisma)
FamilyOffice — top-level tenant (everything scoped to this)
Entity — trust, LLC, SPV, individual; nested tree (self-referential parentId)
Fund — PE/VC/RE/credit fund (manager, vintage, strategy, geography, currency)
Holding — one Entity's position in one Fund
CapitalEvent — CALL or DISTRIBUTION, sourced from a Document
Document — ingested PDF (type, status, s3Key, extraction JSON, workflowId)
DocumentChunk — pgvector embeddings for RAG queries

## Document pipeline (ingest.workflow.ts)
1. Document arrives (upload or email)
2. classifyDocument → CAPITAL_CALL | DISTRIBUTION | NAV_STATEMENT | K1
3. extractDocument → calls extractor → fields[] with { value, confidence: 0-1 }
4. If any field.confidence < 0.85 → notifyReview → await approval (7 days max)
5. storeExtraction → persist Document record with extraction JSON
6. If CAPITAL_CALL or DISTRIBUTION → persistCapitalEvents
7. Store corrections in training dataset if user made edits

## Code standards
TypeScript:
- Strict mode always. No `any` types.
- Money: always Decimal/string in API/DB, never float. Use decimal.js.
- Dates: UTC ISO strings in API responses, DateTime in Prisma.
- EVERY database query MUST include familyOfficeId in WHERE clause.
- Services own business logic. Controllers handle HTTP only.

Python:
- Type hints everywhere.
- All request/response types are Pydantic BaseModel subclasses.
- Each extractor inherits BaseExtractor, implements extract().
- Every extracted field: { "value": Any, "confidence": float }

## Confidence scoring — first-class UX concern
- >= 0.95: auto-approve (green badge)
- 0.85-0.94: accept but surface (blue badge)
- 0.70-0.84: flag for review (amber badge)
- < 0.70: require correction (red badge)
